# ── Private job-search data (git-ignored so a PUBLIC fork can't leak it) ──────
# Every file below holds REAL personal data once you fill it. Each ships as a
# committed `*.example.md` placeholder skeleton; you copy that to the real
# filename here and fill the copy. These real filenames are git-ignored, so the
# documented happy path can never commit your real data to a public repo.
# (In a PRIVATE fork, remove the lines you want to version — see README "Privacy".)

# The structured tracker + generated analysis reports (example: applications.example.md)
references/applications.md
references/pattern-analysis-*.md

# The hand-filled skeletons (each has a committed `*.example.md` to copy from)
references/YOUR_PROFILE.md
references/CUSTOMIZATION_MEMORY.md
references/STRATEGIC_GAP_ASSESSMENT.md
skills/resume-customizer/references/star-stories.md
skills/resume-customizer/references/detailed-context.md
skills/resume-customizer/references/story-bank-index.md

# Your maintained canonical generic resume (a real .docx/.pdf you drop in — see
# resume-customizer STEP 0). Personal, never committed.
references/generic-resume.docx
references/generic-resume.pdf

# ── Generated application documents (the tailored resumes + cover letters) ────
# Every file the `apply` / `resume-customizer` skills generate is written into
# `output/` and holds your real name, history, and the target company. The whole
# directory is git-ignored (only its committed README survives), so a `git add .`
# can never stage a generated application. The filename patterns below are a
# backstop — they keep a generated doc out of git even if you save it elsewhere.
output/*
!output/README.md
*_Resume_*.docx
*_CoverLetter_*.docx
*_Resume_*.pdf
*_CoverLetter_*.pdf

# Any file you rename with a .local.md suffix is also kept out of git — use this
# for any extra personal copy you don't want committed.
*.local.md
